rel-rules-coverage/002-phase-RP2.md\n75\t    sha256: 1218345567781d02ad6cb4d09a2362f6d2436f8e53e6a0946d3bb2b5602847b5\n76\t  - path: .gnd-planning/milestones/pyrel-rules-coverage/003-phase-RP1.md\n77\t    sha256: 6ae343c76554290e99bbff0a3cbbcd1615c75e7233b72a3e456d4865e7b1618d\n78\t  - path: .gnd-planning/milestones/pyrel-rules-coverage/004-phase-RP3.md\n79\t    sha256: 9ebf68eb446056e38ed5d4eb0d0ee1cd5c15563269c2eace78358cd9b5507d6c\n80\t  - path: .gnd-planning/milestones/pyrel-rules-coverage/005-phase-RP4.md\n81\t    sha256: c03a19e798ed63c5fab3df92ce3b3dc762fbd8e7f1a24ca0d0c370287ac95ef7\n82\t  - path: .gnd-planning/milestones/pyrel-rules-coverage/006-phase-RP5.md\n83\t    sha256: 04fe6f4fc7d1197ad7943e0e333f32ed13ab1675b7c6b59a067915be7f9e36ca\n84\t---\n85\t\n86\t# Claim\n87\t\n88\tThe pyrel-rules-coverage milestone closes at these bytes. Issue #214 items\n89\t2–4 are delivered in `gnd export pyrel --rules duckdb|lqp`:\n90\t- external uniqueness;\n91\t- anchored constraints listed as not checked;\n92\t- `Number`;\n93\t- value constraints scoped to a role.\n94\t\n95\tEvery packaged literature model of plan §1 exports, and on every listed\n96\tpopulation the DuckDB program agrees with `gnd check`. Inputs and models\n97\tthe programs cannot represent exactly are refused by name with exit 3; the\n98\truntime page states each refusal. The plan's goal predicates hold\n99\t(`notes/close-results.md`). Plan r4 is amended in §11 (A1–A13); its\n100\tSHA-256 is now `004627d0098184027db692947b4ccb6f2f8cd5c2cc95ad02d3e8a56c142a31af`.\n101\t\n102\t## Phase records\n103\t\n104\t| Phase | Record | Commit |\n105\t|---|---|---|\n106\t| plan gate | `001-plan-gate.md` | `92cd5c9b` |\n107\t| RP2 | `002-phase-RP2.md` | `6e04fe7f` |\n108\t| RP1 | `003-phase-RP1.md` | `1350db9a` |\n109\t| RP3 | `004-phase-RP3.md` | `33bd03db` |\n110\t| RP4 | `005-phase-RP4.md` | `78b9cbb8` |\n111\t| RP5 | `006-phase-RP5.md` | `bba37de8` |\n112\t\n113\t## Close review\n114\t\n115\tInstrument: the full GND panel at xhigh, as the owner selected for plan and\n116\tclose. It ran from a disposable worktree at `e99782fe` over the plan, the\n117\tresults and the delta (`evidence/close-review/`).\n118\t- All six seats returned schema-valid responses; Codex and Gemini blocked,\n119\t  the other four asked for revision. The panel is advisory.\n120\t- Four seats agreed on one blocking concern: the LQP version's exit on an\n121\t  empty disconnected join. Codex raised a second: malformed populations\n122\t  escaped exit 3.\n123\t- All 17 concerns were checked against the code and dispositioned\n124\t  (`disposition.md`): 15 fixed, 2 accepted as residuals.\n125\t- The repairs were read by one independent reviewer (Codex through the\n126\t  review helper, `notes/reviews/close-repairs-r1/`). It found one more\n127\t  rounding route that predates the close (a float literal against an\n128\t  Integer value, now refused, A13) and two faults in the close's own text.\n129\t  All three are fixed.\n130\t- Rounds stop there under the convergence rule (2/3 self-inflicted). The\n131\t  final repair bytes have no further independent reader; the lanes above\n132\t  ran on them.\n133\t\n134\t## Questions swept\n135\t\n136\tAll five open questions of plan §10 are resolved (plan §11, sweep). The\n137\tphase records carry no open question. Residuals accepted:\n138\t- RP3's r5 repair and RP5's last sentence had no second phase reader (the\n139\t  close panel read both).\n140\t- Whole-tree ruff stays red with the known 198 errors, recorded above.\n141\t\n142\t## Close ruling\n143\t\n144\t**Delegated approval (auto=true)**, 2026-11-22, under the owner's\n145\t`/process-milestone pyrel-rules-coverage auto` (2026-11-21), scope this\n146\tmilestone's close. The delegation covers plan, spec and close rulings within\n147\tthis milestone. This ruling is not the owner's reading of the artifacts.\n148\t- Ruled: the goal predicates hold on the evidence above. The plan\n149\t  amendments A1–A13 are accepted, the plan at SHA-256 `004627d0098184027db692947b4ccb6f2f8cd5c2cc95ad02d3e8a56c142a31af`. The\n150\t  residuals listed are accepted.\n151\t- Budget used: the selected panel (plan review two rounds, close one\n152\t  round), plus Codex phase reviews through the helper; no other paid call.\n153\t- Not covered by this ruling: merge, publication and any release, which\n154\t  stay with the owner.\n155\t\n156\t## Boundary — what this close does NOT establish\n157\t\n158\t- No RelationalAI engine ran. The LQP programs compile (oracle lane), but\n159\t  their enforcement on an engine is unverified.\n160\t- Agreement is with `gnd check` without an anchor sidecar.\n161\t- The programs are stricter than GND where stated (`close-results.md`,\n162\t  Boundaries).\n163\t- Nothing is merged or published: the work is on branch\n164\t  `claude/pyrel-rules-coverage`, submitted as a pull request for the\n165\t  owner's decision."}
{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_x2lDQFCHJ78YgsxFJStY8SbW","content":"1\t# DB1 · Correlated guarded existence\n2\t\n3\tAwaiting owner review.\n4\t\n5\t## 2. Provenance\n6\t\n7\t[Plan r1](duckdb-rule-checking-plan.md), §§0–10, based on main 83cd4170 and [#330](https://github.com/vasiloglou/gnd-public/issues/330). Draft dated 2026-11-27. Plan and spec have received no independent review.\n8\t\n9\t## 3. Harvested obligations\n10\t\n11\t| Source | Obligation | Discharge |\n12\t|---|---|---|\n13\t| Plan §4, DB1 | Compile a guarded existential over a joint assignment and expose violating antecedent bindings. | Design §6 and acceptance §8 |\n14\t| Plan §§0, 6 | Use a read-only engine transaction. Validate relation identifiers and projection types before executing a rule. Bound counterexample rows independently of scan size. | Boundary cases in §8 and focused regression suite |\n15\t| Plan §§5, 9 | Preserve current caller behavior and document the new contract | Reference and compatibility control in §§6, 8 |\n16\t\n17\t## 4. Standing rules as applied\n18\t\n19\tThe boundary under test is [src/gnd/export/pyrel_rules.py](../../../../src/gnd/export/pyrel_rules.py). Validate structural inputs before computing a result. Use a read-only engine transaction. Validate relation identifiers and projection types before executing a rule. Bound counterexample rows independently of scan size. Use one traversal for all supported declarations or bindings. Compare the issue fixture and a differently named or reordered equivalent. The normative reference is [docs/reference/capabilities.md](../../../../docs/reference/capabilities.md). Record measured work in the fixture wherever a budget affects results.\n20\t\n21\t## 5. Goal\n22\t\n23\tCompile a guarded existential over a joint assignment and expose violating antecedent bindings. The focused selection exits 0 with every branch below exercised.\n24\t\n25\t## 6. Design proposal\n26\t\n27\tRetain gnd export pyrel MODEL --rules duckdb --out FILE. Bind the antecedent once, correlate shared variables into a single existential join, and project antecedent identities for which no joint tuple exists. Apply ordinary coverage checks before computing absence.\n28\t\n29\tReuse the existing components export.pyrel_rules, check.service, check.constraint_families and add the contract to [docs/reference/capabilities.md](../../../../docs/reference/capabilities.md). Implement the change at the defining layer and retain existing public call paths. Baseline control: The guarded rule fixture passes native checking with an approved outcome and fails with an unrelated one. PyRel export currently refuses an existential consequent. A SQLite cursor is rejected by api.check.\n30\t\n31\tIn `export/pyrel_rules.py`, follow the guarded consequent dispatch and rule-block emitter. Reuse its model-derived names and requirement identities. Bind consequent-only variables inside the joined relation. An empty consequent match is a violation only for an applicable, covered antecedent binding. Verify a variable shared by two consequent atoms, as well as one shared with the antecedent.\n32\t\n33\t## 7. Owner decisions\n34\t\n35\tD1 (open). Approve the guarded fragment and native execution environment. DB2 must choose an explicit NULL mapping and transaction identity before accepting relation bindings.\n36\t\n37\t## 8. Tests and expected results\n38\t\n39\tOne matching outcome passes; an approved value belonging to another request fails. Two atoms satisfied by distinct values cannot satisfy one shared variable. Covered-empty and uncovered inputs produce the native verdict in each case. Other unsupported consequents retain the existing refusal.\n40\t\n41\tAdd the new cases in `tests/test_duckdb_rule_checking_phase1.py`. Preserve [tests/test_pyrel_rules.py](../../../../tests/test_pyrel_rules.py) as an affected-consumer regression. Proposed command after implementation:\n42\t\n43\t```sh\n44\tuv run pytest -q tests/test_duckdb_rule_checking_phase1.py tests/test_pyrel_rules.py --tb=short\n45\t```\n46\t\n47\tExpected pytest exit 0. Compare the old public path on the same unchanged input and record its verdict or emitted bytes.\n48\t\n49\t## 9. Phase boundary\n50\t\n51\tLater phases own read-only relation bindings, backend parity. Other SQL engines and general SQL compilation remains outside the milestone.\n52\t\n53\t## 10. Size estimate\n54\t\n55\tEstimated 130–260 source lines and 120–230 test lines. Measure the actual diff in the phase record.\n56\t\n57\t## 11. Approval instrument\n58\t\n59\tThis draft and its case expectations are the proposed approval instrument. Record an owner decision on the plan and this spec before phase execution."}
